Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Trauma Surgeon (Lead)): Specializes in the immediate management of penetrating chest injuries, particularly focusing on the surgical intervention required for the right ventricular injury and bleeding control. This member leads the group due to their critical role in carrying out the necessary surgical procedure.
 Member 2 (Emergency Physician): Responsible for the initial trauma assessment and resuscitation efforts, including fluid management and monitoring vital signs. This member supports the trauma surgeon by ensuring appropriate stabilization of the patient.
 Member 3 (Anesthesiologist): Focuses on perioperative care and ensuring proper anesthesia for the impending surgical intervention. This member will manage sedation and any potential complications arising from intubation and anesthesia during the surgery.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Cardiothoracic Surgeon (Lead)): Provides expertise on surgical approaches to repair the right ventricular free wall injury and addresses any potential complications related to cardiac surgery. They play a key role in assessing the need for surgical intervention versus other methods.
 Member 2 (Sonographer or Echocardiograph Technician): Conducts transthoracic echocardiography and assists in the imaging evaluation of cardiac injuries to ensure accurate assessments are made for surgical planning.
 Member 3 (Radiologist): Interprets imaging studies and provides critical input on the anatomical considerations surrounding the penetrating injury, as well as identifying any associated injuries that may not be immediately apparent.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Trauma Consultant (Lead)): Offers overarching expertise in trauma care and guides the decision
 Member 2 (Clinical Decision Specialist): Coordinates information from the Initial Assessment Team and Diagnostic Evidence Team, synthesizing the data to formulate a clear treatment plan while also considering alternative options, including the risks and benefits of each.
 Member 3 (Hospital Administrator or Trauma Program Coordinator): Ensures that the necessary logistics and support systems are in place for the surgical intervention, including preparation of the operating room and coordination of multidisciplinary resources during the patient’s care pathway.

